**Convergence**

Holstonia did not begin as a sudden idea. It emerged gradually at the intersection of experiences that, for many years, appeared unrelated.

Across my professional and avocational life, a common discipline was forming — one grounded in careful observation, structured record-keeping, and respect for evidence.

Work in ornithology cultivated habits of patient watching: learning that meaningful patterns often reveal themselves only across time, and that observer effort is itself a measurable variable. Field environments taught humility before complexity and reinforced the difference between noticing something and understanding it.

Archaeological involvement deepened that perspective. Archaeology is, in many ways, the science of disciplined inference — the reconstruction of reality from incomplete traces. It demands methodological restraint, contextual thinking, and an awareness that absence can be as informative as presence.

Years spent in the newspaper profession added another layer: the obligation to separate signal from rumor, to verify before concluding, and to recognize how narratives form and spread within communities.

Later work within the court system strengthened habits of procedural rigor — documentation, chain of custody, evidentiary standards, and the understanding that claims must withstand scrutiny beyond the moment in which they are made.

Alongside these professional paths ran a lifelong engagement with the natural world: seasons, terrain, weather, animal movement, and the quiet variability of Appalachian landscapes. Over time, these experiences fostered a particular comfort with uncertainty — not as a void to be filled quickly, but as a condition within which disciplined inquiry becomes possible.

What eventually became clear was that these were not separate pursuits. They were converging competencies.

Holstonia is the outward expression of that convergence.

It reflects the belief that even subjects surrounded by ambiguity can be approached with methodological seriousness; that careful structure can exist without premature conclusion; and that observation, when guided by discipline, remains one of the most reliable instruments available to us.

This work is therefore less a departure than a continuation — the natural meeting point of a lifetime spent learning how to look closely, record faithfully, and ask better questions.
